                    briefing_title_r1:      `Round 1 — Diagnose the transformation need, not the stated request`,
                    briefing_text_r1:       `Veridian's brief says “train 300 managers.” The real mandate is to <strong>dismantle the legacy-P&L resistance</strong> that has stalled the services pivot for 18 months and to build genuine services conviction. Separate the <em>stated request</em> from the <em>transformation need</em>, then name the single headline success metric you will defend to all three sponsors. The trap that got Atelier Cadence fired before: accept the brief at face value, design a beautiful academy, and watch the same pulse survey come back unchanged. What barrier were you actually paid to remove?`,
                    briefing_title_r2:      `Round 2 — Co-design the programme architecture under the €840k cap`,
                    briefing_text_r2:       `Turn the diagnosis into a learning architecture. First, the <strong>co-creation mechanism</strong> — do Veridian's own SMEs and senior leaders enter the design room and co-teach, or do your faculty build and deliver everything? Co-creation is the value proposition, not a nicety; off-the-shelf is €200k cheaper, so you must be worth the premium. Then allocate the full €840k across formats using <strong>70-20-10 logic</strong>: on-the-job application (70) and coaching (20) buy behaviour change; classroom intensives (10) buy satisfaction. Spend everything on classrooms and you rebuild the €1.6M smile-sheet that got you fired.`,
                    briefing_title_r3:      `Round 3 — Negotiate scope, metrics & the steering committee`,
                    briefing_text_r3:       `Face the steering committee live. The CHRO buys behaviour change, the COO buys a services-revenue lift, the CFO buys completion and cost. <strong>Reconcile the three into one balanced scorecard</strong> — satisfaction, transformation impact, budget adherence and completion together — or you will please one sponsor and lose the other two. The consultancy's cheaper bid is leverage against you: <em>defend the co-creation premium</em> or get dragged into a price war that strips out the behaviour change. And speed-to-trust is part of the design — take four weeks to perfect it and Veridian signs the consultancy in week three.`,
                    briefing_title_r4:      `Round 4 — Commit, measure & de-risk delivery`,
                    briefing_text_r4:       `Pitch the CEO and pre-empt failure. Define the <strong>impact-measurement plan</strong>: evidence behaviour and business outcomes at <strong>3, 6 and 9 months</strong>, not just smile-sheets at course end — Veridian's FY27 budget meeting will ask “did it work?” Build the <strong>renewal logic</strong> that turns one cohort into a multi-year relationship, and a <strong>risk register</strong> for the real failure modes: busy senior managers not completing, legacy-P&L managers gaming attendance, and the 16-week launch slipping. The trap: launch on week 16 with attendance logs and happy faces, and no evidence the pivot will be funded.`,

