                    coach_default:          `Decision logged.`,
                    coach_r1_need_right:    `<strong>That's the real mandate.</strong> Veridian asked you to train 300 managers, but they are paying you to dismantle the legacy-P&L resistance and build services conviction. Now you can design the intervention they actually need instead of the academy that gets quietly shelved.`,
                    coach_r1_train:         `<strong>You've accepted the brief at face value.</strong> Eighteen months from now Veridian runs the same pulse survey: the legacy-P&L managers still protect hardware, services revenue is flat, and your satisfaction score is 4.6 out of 5. That is exactly the €1.6M logistics academy that got Atelier Cadence fired. What barrier were you actually paid to remove?`,
                    coach_r1_upskill:       `<strong>A commercial-skills gap is part of it — but not the heart of it.</strong> You can teach subscription-economy skills perfectly and still lose, because the legacy-P&L managers don't want the pivot to succeed. This is a political resistance problem wearing a skills costume.`,
                    coach_r1_comms:         `<strong>Better communication won't move people who are protecting their P&L.</strong> Leadership has communicated for 18 months; the managers heard it and resisted anyway. The barrier is incentive and identity, not message clarity.`,
                    coach_r1_diag_sharp:    `<strong>Sharp diagnosis.</strong> You named the real transformation need AND chose a behaviour/business-outcome metric you can defend to all three sponsors. That is the lens that lets you design for results, not smile-sheets — and that all three sponsors can sign.`,
                    coach_r1_completion:    `<strong>Completion is the CFO's number, not the transformation.</strong> You can hit 95% completion and change nothing. The CHRO will ask what behaviour changed and the COO what revenue lifted — and a completion headline answers neither.`,
                    coach_r1_satisfaction:  `<strong>Satisfaction is the smile-sheet that got you fired.</strong> The €1.6M academy scored 4.6 out of 5 and changed no behaviour. Above Kirkpatrick Level 1, satisfaction predicts almost nothing about the business outcome.`,
                    coach_r1_attendance:    `<strong>Attendance hours measure presence, not change.</strong> Three hundred managers can sit through every session and still protect the legacy P&L. None of your three sponsors is buying attendance.`,
                    coach_r1_lowcandour:    `<strong>If you won't name the political barrier, you'll design around it.</strong> Soft-pedalling the legacy-P&L resistance produces a polite training plan that leaves the real blocker untouched — and repeats the lapse.`,
                    coach_r2_codesign:      `<strong>Co-design is the value proposition.</strong> Putting Veridian's SMEs in the design room and their senior leaders into live action-learning means the programme is owned, not rented — and answers the COO's question, “where are our people in this?”`,
                    coach_r2_advisory:      `<strong>An advisory board is co-creation theatre.</strong> Sign-off is not the same as co-design; the work is still yours and the ownership stays yours. When you leave, who owns the change? Off-the-shelf is €200k cheaper — why pay your premium for a design Veridian didn't help build?`,
                    coach_r2_inhouse:       `<strong>“So this is your academy that we pay for?”</strong> An all-in-house design repeats the exact €1.6M mistake — high polish, no ownership, no behaviour change. Co-creation isn't a nicety here; it's the only reason to choose you over the cheaper consultancy.`,
                    coach_r2_balanced:      `<strong>That's a 70-20-10 architecture, co-created.</strong> On-the-job application and coaching carry the behaviour change, classroom instruction supports it, and Veridian's own people are in the room. This is what buys Level-3/4 impact instead of a smile-sheet.`,
                    coach_r2_classheavy:    `<strong>You've bought satisfaction, not behaviour change.</strong> A classroom-heavy intensive will score beautifully on the smile-sheet and change almost nothing on the job — the precise €1.6M trap. Shift budget into on-the-job application and coaching.`,
                    coach_r2_digitalheavy:  `<strong>Cheap digital self-paced buys completion, not conviction.</strong> Leaning on a self-paced platform protects margin and click-through rates, but resistant managers tick boxes without changing behaviour. The “70” has to be real applied work.`,
                    coach_r3_balanced:      `<strong>One balanced scorecard aligns three sponsors.</strong> Satisfaction, transformation impact, budget adherence and completion together — now the CHRO, COO and CFO are buying the same programme. That is the negotiation artefact that wins all three.`,
                    coach_r3_cfo:           `<strong>You've optimised the CFO's KPI and lost the other two.</strong> Cost-per-participant down to €1,900 and 95% completion — the CFO loves you. But the CHRO has no behaviour answer and the COO no revenue answer. You've sold a cheap course, not a transformation, and two of three sponsors won't renew.`,
                    coach_r3_chro:          `<strong>Leading only with the CHRO unbalances you the other way.</strong> Maximising behaviour change while waving away cost and completion hands the CFO a reason to claw back and the consultancy a clean shot. The scorecard has to hold all four corners.`,
                    coach_r3_defer:         `<strong>Designing to whoever shouts loudest is not negotiation.</strong> Letting the committee fight it out means the loudest sponsor wins and the design lurches round-to-round. Bring one balanced scorecard and make them align to it.`,
                    coach_r3_premium:       `<strong>You defended the co-creation premium.</strong> You gave Veridian a concrete reason designing WITH them beats a cheaper rented academy — ownership, fit, and a measured business outcome. That is how you justify the price and the wait.`,
                    coach_r3_match:         `<strong>Matching their price strips out the change.</strong> Cutting action-learning and coaching to undercut the consultancy protects margin but removes the “70” that buys behaviour change — you become the cheaper academy you were supposed to beat.`,
                    coach_r3_ignore:        `<strong>Ignoring the competing bid is a commercial risk.</strong> Your design may be better, but if nobody gives Veridian a reason co-design is worth the premium and the wait, they default to cheaper-and-faster. Speed-to-trust is part of the design.`,
                    coach_r3_slow:          `<strong>You took too long.</strong> While you perfected the proposal, Veridian signed the consultancy's off-the-shelf academy in week three — cheaper, faster, and nobody from your team had defended the co-creation premium. You've lost the mandate and the reference logo.`,
                    coach_r3_uniform:       `<strong>One uniform format can't fit 300 managers across 3 bands and 4 languages.</strong> A single design lands well for some and badly for others. Differentiating depth and format by seniority and language is what makes it stick.`,
                    coach_r4_checkpoints:   `<strong>Measurement designed in from the start.</strong> Evidencing behaviour and business outcomes at 3, 6 and 9 months means Veridian's FY27 meeting can actually judge whether it worked — and gives you the renewal case. That is impact above Level 1.`,
                    coach_r4_endcourse:     `<strong>End-of-course evaluation is too late and too shallow.</strong> Satisfaction and completion at the finish tell you nothing about behaviour at month six. When FY27 asks “did it work?”, you'll have happy faces and no evidence.`,
                    coach_r4_nomeasure:     `<strong>Launch without measurement is the classic decay pattern.</strong> You launch on week 16 — congratulations. Nine months later FY27 asks if it worked and you have attendance logs and no evidence of behaviour or business change. The services pivot gets shelved, and so does your renewal.`,
                    coach_r4_multiyear:     `<strong>That's a multi-year relationship, not a one-off cohort.</strong> A second-cohort and alumni-faculty plan tied to the FY27 funding decision turns this mandate into the reference logo and the recurring revenue Atelier Cadence needs.`,
                    coach_r4_norenewal:     `<strong>No renewal logic leaves value on the table.</strong> Delivering one cohort well and moving on means you re-pitch from scratch next time. Design the renewal into the measurement plan so one cohort becomes a programme.`,
                    coach_r4_gaming:        `<strong>Tying progress to real P&L deliverables beats gaming.</strong> Legacy-P&L managers who treat the programme as attendance theatre can't fake a delivered project on their own P&L — that's how you turn resistant managers from a learning audience into accountable owners.`,
                    coach_overrun:          `<strong>You've blown the €840k cap.</strong> The CFO ring-fenced 840 and claws back overruns — and remembers who ignored the envelope. Your margin just fell below the line and the CFO is no longer aligned. Which formats do you cut to protect behaviour change while losing the overspend?`,

                    sum_default:            `Decision made.`,
